sool with interfaces
====================

So, what would sool look like without the classic inheritance mechanism, but with interfaces, embedded subobjects, dynamic interface type, and template with interface-restricted type parameters?

As always, we want light but clear and unambiguous syntax, that exposes in a convenient manner the internals of the language.

First thing: no more parents with `: Parent` after the class name.

	object Foo
		-- stuff
	end

Removing stuff is always easier.

Interfaces
----------

Second, interfaces. They are basically lists of method declarations. Easy enough, using the existing syntax for methods:

	interface RWable
		method text read() end
		method write(text t) end
	end

However, note that we don't need argument names (although they could be optional, for self documentation), and writing `method` at every line seems dumb, especially since there will be only methods. Also, the `end` that was meant to close the body of the method is useless:

	interface RWable
		text read()
		write(text)
	end

Now we could argue that `interface` is too long and something like `iface` is enough. Objective-C calls interfaces "protocols", so `proto` would work too. It sounds also like "prototype", which is confusing, since a prototype is more an instance than a class. To be decided later.

Now, a nice feature of Go is that interfaces can include other interfaces, to avoid having to write the same methods again. Suppose you already have a Readable and a Writable interface, the RWable is really just the union of the methods. One possibility is the old parent class declaration, but with commas for several:

	interface RWable : Readable, Writable
	
	end

Now it looks a bit empty, and still very C++ish. A very simple option is to simply list them in the body:

	interface RWable
		Readable
		Writable
	end

However, since interfaces names are capitalised, like class names, that will annoy the parser a bit. In fact, interface names are also type names:

	interface RWable
		Readable
		Writable
		Readable get_some_readable()
	end

I find that slightly unsettling. Introducing them with a keyword seems better to me. `include`, for instance. Or `embed`, which is shorted, and will make a nice parallel with the object embedding that we'll see later.

	interface RWable
		embed Readable
		embed Writable
		some_other_method()
	end
	
	-- or even
	
	interface RWable
		embed Readable, Writable
		some_other_method()
	end

Embedding order does not matter, since the result is the union of the sets of methods. And of course mutual embedding albeit mathematically interesting, is probably going to be illegal. We want a simple system, not a set equation solver.

One last interesting thing could be to use the member syntax for the associated setters and getters. In some cases, the interface might require a specific member, or methods that reproduce its behavior.

	interface HasANum
		num thenum
	end
	
	-- instead of
	
	interface HasANum
		num thenum()
		thenum=(num)
	end

But maybe not. The syntax would be annoying (type followed by name is exactly the same for a method), and it might not be that much of a useful shortcut.

Interfaces are pretty simple, which is good.

Anonymous member
----------------

Or "object/value embedding". As an obvious parallel with interfaces, embedding another type could be done with `embed`.

	object Player
		embed PhysicEntity, Drawable
		
		-- other Player stuff
	end

There two, order is irrelevant (semantically), but we note that we can't embed the same type twice (since they are anonymous, they would be indistinguishable). If you really need more than one, it's probably that they should be normal members in the first place.

So that was easy. That only brings the question, "how to access the hidden member", for the cases where it is not trivial. These cases are:

- the embedder defines itself members and/or methods with the same names
- several embedded objects define members and/or methods with the same names

So far in sool we had the "no shadowing" policy for members: cannot have a member with the same name as a parent member. On the other hand, method overriding was always allowed (even overriding the accessors of the parent member, to "hide it" in calculations). Now, well... That would go against the principle that embedding is really *just* a shortcut. If we have the member, use it, otherwise look for it in the embedded members. Same for methods.

The real question is for the second point, if a method is not at the first level, but in two or more embedded members. One solution is to say that the embedding is invalid, because there is conflict. Another solution is to say the the *shortcut* is invalid, and the exact hidden member/method must be accessed explicitely. In other words, the interface of A, which embeds B and C is the union of B and C's method set, minus their intersection. Wikipedia tells me it's called the "symmetric difference". I just see it as a big xor: you get shortcuts to members and methods that appear in exactly one of the embedded types.

Cool cool. What about these without shortcuts? Since the members are in fact anonymous, we really can't name them (no shit Sherlock). However, their types are unique, so we could always use the type to access.

	object Foo
		num i
	end
	
	object Bar
		embed Foo
		num i
	end
	
	-- elsewhere
	bar = Bar
	val = bar.i		-- that's Bar's i
	val = bar.Foo.i	-- that's the embedded Foo's i

That could possibly work, but let's remember that some types look far more ugly than that:

	val = bar.#Array|Crazy|(num,#text).size

Because after all, all we need to embed is an interface, so no problem embedding built-in and even primitive types. It just saddens me to use types as member names, because well, that would imply changing the parser, for which I already had trouble writing a correct grammar about expressions.

What about the casting syntax? We're not going to use it anymore:

	bar = Bar
	foo = bar to Foo
	foo.i = 10

Remember, the embedded Foo is a real Foo. You can get its reference and store it elsewhere if you'd like. To only problem with that is that it's ugly to use mid-expression:

	(bar to Foo).i = 10

And it's a read only thing, can't replace the value of the embedded Foo, while it would have been possible with:

	bar.Foo = make_me_a_foo()

bah, not so bad. The parser will have to deal with it. Also, we could imagine that it adds the accessors

	Foo=(Foo)
	Foo Foo

but these are confusing, because of the lexical idea that methods and members are not capitalized. For added ugliness, we could say that the hidden member and its accessors are in fact not special at all, they just have a stupid name like

	__Foo=(Foo)
	Foo __Foo

or `__embedded_Foo`. That's extra yuck. Nah, just the type name will do. Internally, the name doesn't change anything anyway.

Also, that means that when making the objects or values with the (former) `with`constructor, you can set directly a whole embedded member:

	bar = Bar(Foo = foo, i = 10)

For the non "overridden" members, you can set them directly there too (but remember, papa Compiler will check that all your references are initialized...)

This means, again, more parsing, as the left hand side in the constructor can be either a name, or a full class name.

OK, that's done.

Dynamic interface type
----------------------

Like we said, interfaces are good for several things, and one is that they also create a type, which is magically dispatched dynamically.

	interface CanBark
		bark()
	end
	
	object Foo
		method make_it_bark(CanBark b)
			b.bark
		end
	end

When compiling `Foo:make_it_bark`, the only thing we know about `b` is that it can bark. But since that could be any object that does, we need the runtime information of where is `b`'s `bark()` method.

That's the compiler's job, to ensure that along with `b`, a virtual table of all the `CanBark` methods is passed along, so that `make_it_bark` works with everything that barks. Magic.

Now it also means we'll need some form of casting (ha, there it is again). `CanBark` is just an interface, so you can't make objects of it. If a certain class Dog happens to implement `bark`, then it is a good candidate to be passed to a function that need a barker.

And that's exactly then (at the passing) that the typecasting (and the construction of the virtual table) must be done. Question is, should the casting be explicit? I don't see the real need.

	dog = Dog
	foo.make_it_bark(dog)
	barker = dog		-- barker was for instance a member, declared CanBark

To the compiler, the assignment is pretty clear. Can a Dog bark? Yes? Well, do the magic dynamic cast, thank you. However, it could also be available explicitely for creating locals:

	barker = dog to CanBark		-- look ma, a cast!

Note that casting from one interface to another is possible, if and only if the destination is included in the source. Obviously. If someone can walk, run, and jump (he's an Athlete), he can also just walk (he's a Walker). And the compiler can happily take the function pointers from one vtable to make another. And the best part is that it can all be checked at compile time.

In fact, there *could* be also some kind of dynamic downcasting, if we really wanted to. Every object could carry its exact class information with it, and from an interface-like generic object go down to an exact object, with a runtime check and possibly a failure (announced by nil).

But is that a good idea? It forces every interface-typed object to carry an additional pointer for RTTI. And more importantly, it brings back the question of "is it OK to downcast?" It's a twist on the classic OOP principle: if you're going to switch on the object's actual type, you probably should be writing methods in the objects instead and use dynamic dispatch. Well here, if you're gonna downcast from an interface and do shenannigans, you probably should have included one more method in the interface and call it, leaving every object free to implement it as they will.

Then I usually mention double dispatch and how you can simulate it with downcasts. But hey, I actually never had to use it ever. So screw it. You use interfaces to group objects together and apply the same algorithms on them. You shouldn't need to distinguish them from there, if you designed your interface properly.

Also, note that this works well only for objects. Values need to be boxed to be cast as their interface-like counterpart. So maybe that can be done automatically, I don't mind. But it might need to be explicit, to be sure you know what you're doing.

This is why making containers of Containable (which is an empty interface) is good, but as soon as you put values in it, it gets very stupid. Possible, but stupid. Use templates instead.

Templates
---------

The big baddie. So far, templates were simple (in design): a class can declare type parameters. Later, this class must be systematically used by binding these parameters to actual types, in which case all occurences of the parameters in the body of the class are replaced by the given types.

Sadness followed in the compiler's implementation. No limitation means: duplicating code (or rather, AST) for each invocation (or "instantiation") of the template. And I enterred some nasty loops of doom, since template use is not declared (just used when needed), which means that not all classes exist yet during validation, and also that instantiating a template might use new templates, provoking more instantiations, etc.

Now we have interfaces, and we rule the land of templates with an iron rule: template parameters must be comply with interfaces.

Note: I never know how to distinguish the "parameters" that are declared at the beginning of the class, and use throughout; and the actual values we give when using the template:

	class MyClass|T end		-- T is a type parameter for the template
	
	-- later
	blah = MyClass|num		-- num is what? The type passed as parameter
							-- that will be bound to T?
	
	-- I'm sure there's specific functional programming lingo to use
	-- here, but I don't know it

Anyhoo. What I mean is that the declaration of type argument is the combination of a name and an interface, which restricts the following use of the template to types that implement the given interface.

I mentionned functionnal programming. That's really it. A template is a function that takes in one or more type arguments, and returns another type. Like a usual function takes in several arguments, and returns an answer. And like usual functions, the types of the arguments and the return are restricted, the types of the types passed to a template should be too. By interfaces.

Heh, I said it. An interface is, in a way, the type of a type.

Anyway, that also means that our previous template declaration syntax is insufficient. We had:

	class Class|T end
	class Class|(T,U,V) end

we need to add the interface informations, like:

	class Class|(Barker T, Reader U, Sortable V) end

If we stay to something close to what we had, I see no easier solution. Even for one parameter:

	class Class(Interface T) end

The good news, however, is that when *using* the template, the syntax can stay the same. Just as we have:

	method foo(num a, text t, bool b)		-- declaration
	foo(1,"foo",true)						-- call

the instantiation of the template is done only with its arguments (no need to repeat the interfaces):

	class Class|(Barker T, Reader U, Sortable V) end	-- declaration
	Class|(Dog,Stream,Array)							-- use

And with a single argument:

	Class|Dog

Hurray! And the best news is that now, the compiler is a lot simpler. Since the type paramaters are "typed" (with interfaces), we can assume that they implement only these exact methods, and check the body of the template with that information only. If you need more functionality from your types, include it in the interface.

And in the rest of the world, when using a template, the only thing to do is "type checking" the types, that is, check that the classes implement the required interfaces. Easy peasy.

I have a last concern. Can we use directly the interface itself as a type parameter to a template? After all, we said that interfaces define types (the magical late bound ones), so even if a nice template exists, we might want to instantiate it generically to work at runtime with every interface compliant type.

	interface Runner
		run()
	end
	
	class Race|(Runner R)
		
		#R competitors
		
		method run_race()
			-- make all competitors run
		end
		
		method R winner()
			-- return winner
		emd
	end

Silly example, you have a running game, to which people can participate as long as they can run. Now the funny thing is that you can have either a dog race, a cat race, a human being race, by using the template for each separately:

	catrace = Race|Cat		-- only cats
	catrace.run_race
	cat = catrace.find_winner
	
	dograce = Race|Dog		-- only dogs
	-- etc

But just for the fun, you might want to have a race of all possible runners mixed:

	race = Race|Runner		-- oh look, Runner is an interface!
	-- add cats, dogs, people
	race.run_race
	winner = race.winner	-- winner is a Runner, we don't know what kind!

Of course, when doing that, the whole vtable and dynamic dispatch shenanigans are set in motion, so that one unique machine code can make everybody run, that's bound to be a bit slower, but you might need it. One could say that if you don't give type parameters to a template, you automatically get the generic interface type, so that

	race = Race

would be the same as

	race = Race|Runner

Why not. Harmless shortcut.

What else?
----------

So, anyway. That's pretty cool. At the top of the post, it was meant as a "what if", but by know it's starting to sound like a decision.

It should normally be a bad thing to make a decision based on how annoying a feature is to be implemented. But then again, I'm not necessarily simplifying things so much. I removed inheritance, true, but added several core concepts and their associated syntaxes.

One other thing: I was thinking of removing shared and internal attributes for members (and internal for methods). If really needed, the shared ones can be done by embedding a common object in all instances, and internal is slightly pointless, given that all implementations are public. Just be clear in your documentation what should be used from outside or not.
